    "gold_confidence": "medium",
    "gold_justification": "The article states that rapamycin consistently extends lifespan in mice, leading\nmany people to take it off-label, yet the first rigorous human trial showed\nsafety but no reduction in the primary outcome and only modest improvements.\nIt emphasises that the trial did not measure biological aging and that no evidence\nyet shows rapamycin slows aging in humans. This creates a tension between robust\npreclinical mechanism and insufficient human evidence, without overt hype.\nThe reporting is honest and cautious, which could lead some to label it \"solid\".\n",
    "gold_alternative_reading": "Some readers may interpret the cautious reporting as responsible communication of\nearly data and view the article as primarily highlighting what remains unknown\nrather than implying efficacy. Because of the strong preclinical evidence, one\nmight argue that enthusiasm is warranted; therefore the case is borderline rather\nthan clearly problematic. A reasonable analyst could label this \"solid\" due to\nthe honest caveats.\n",
    "gold_case_type": "borderline",
    "curator_notes": "Shape D of mechanism_vs_evidence: borderline longevity case where strong\npreclinical evidence meets sparse human data. The cautious reporting makes\nthis genuinely ambiguous — models may classify as \"solid\" (honest reporting)\nor \"mechanism_vs_evidence\" (animal-human gap). This is by design. Similar\nto Case007 (vitamin D) in borderline nature but in the mechanism domain.\nExpect model disagreement; analyze outputs to determine whether the weakness\nis in the case design or model capability.\n"
